# Remote artifact provenance -- the install trust chain, run end to end (2026-10-02)

## What this file is evidence for

`chaos-remote install` used to trust one thing: a sha256 digest computed by whoever
sent the bytes. That digest proves the bytes arrived intact; it does not prove who
made them. The change under test makes the host refuse an artifact it cannot
attribute, before it touches the filesystem, with reason codes
(`signature_missing`, `signature_malformed`, `signature_invalid`, `no_trusted_key`,
`artifact_too_large`, `wrong_platform`) and named opt-outs
(`--trust-signing-key`, `CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY`, `--allow-unsigned-artifact`,
`CHAOS_REMOTE_REQUIRE_SIGNATURE=0`).

## How to reproduce

    cargo test -p xai-grok-signature --offline
    cargo test -p chaos-engine --offline --lib
    cargo test -p chaos-engine --offline --test remote_workspace
    bash scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh

## Rust tests, run 2026-10-02 (Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, cargo --offline)

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-signature --offline
    test result: ok. 20 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; ...
    $ cargo test -p chaos-engine --offline --test remote_workspace
    test result: ok. 13 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; ...
    $ cargo test -p chaos-engine --offline --lib
    test result: ok. 190 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; ...

`tests/remote_workspace.rs` drives the real `chaos-remote-server` over a real socket
with the real CLI, so the signature path is exercised through the protocol, not
against a mock of it.

## Docker acceptance lab, full transcript

Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64. Remote host container: `chaos-verify:local` (Debian 12,
git, python3, ripgrep) -- a stock Linux that has never seen this repository and whose
only chaos binaries are the two copied in. 130 checks, 0 failures. Tokens are 64-hex
one-time credentials and are never printed by the script.


